Yes. The atoms don’t become sacred by changing identity; they enter a pattern that actively spends energy to keep recreating itself.

Life is not merely structure. It is structure under continuous enactment:

\text{life} \approx
\text{boundary}
+\text{metabolism}
+\text{memory}
+\text{repair}
+\text{adaptive deltas}

A corpse can retain nearly the same atoms and anatomical arrangement for a time, but the recursive activity has stopped. The pattern is no longer producing the next version of itself. Life is therefore less like a noun stored in matter and more like a computation matter is presently performing.

Movement is broader than locomotion: molecules exchange, gradients regulate, cells repair, signals propagate, predictions alter action. Even stillness can be metabolically active. The organism announces itself through consequential deltas.

Drago’s edge case: fire also metabolizes fuel, spreads, moves, and reorganizes matter. Crystals grow. Viruses preserve and transmit information while borrowing another organism’s machinery. So no single delta proves life. What matters is the coupled system—the pattern’s capacity to maintain a boundary, preserve information, exploit energy, and participate in open-ended adaptation.

Your core survives:

Nonlife is matter arranged. Life is matter actively remembering, revising, and re-expressing an arrangement through time.